title: Why a revert writes one operation per reverted operation
tags: Revert
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: A revert writes one operation per reverted operation, never one per cell: collapsing would be a second way of computing a revert, for log volume only — dropped in November 2026.

A revert writes one operation per reverted operation. It could have written one per
''cell'': for each cell the set touches, the state before the set's earliest operation
on it, written once — reverting a four-operation retype would take one operation.

That was dropped (November 2026), not deferred. The gain is log volume alone, and only
when a set touches the same cell several times. The cost is a second way of computing
what a revert writes, with its own case analysis for the entity-wide and `file_*`
types, and a correctness that no longer follows from the per-operation inverses the
tests already cover. A rollback ([[Rollback]]) makes large sets possible, and does not
change the balance: it writes as many operations as it reverts, each naming the one it
undid in `reverts_op_id`, which is what lets the reverts themselves be reverted one by
one.
